  "question": {
    "text": "<p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math> moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BaCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is mixed with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></math> the maximum number of&nbsp;moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Ba</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></math>formed is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi></math>&nbsp;(Nearest integer)</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "1",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The balanced chemical equation for the reaction between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BaCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> is:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>BaCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>Ba</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>NaCl</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;moles of barium chloride will require&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;moles of sodium phosphate.</p><p>Here, sodium phosphate is the limiting reagent.<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;moles of sodium phosphate produce&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;mole of barium phosphate.</p><p>Thus,<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;moles of sodium phosphate will produce<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;mole of barium phosphate.</p><p>Hence, the maximum no. of moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Ba</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mo>)</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub></math>that can be formed is one&nbsp;mole.</p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
  },
